WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.820
It is a pleasure to welcome back to the program.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.009
Sean Carroll, who is a theoretical physicist,

00:00:06.009 --> 00:00:12.000
cal tech host of the podcast mindscape and
also author of the forthcoming book, something

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.740
deeply hidden, which is out in September.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.990
Um, maybe we start there, Sean.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.610
I mean, what is it to be with this idea of
something being hidden is very interesting

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:22.950
when we talk about your work.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:26.180
What is it that you allude to there as being
hidden from us?

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:31.560
Well, it's a quote from Einstein actually,
and the book is about quantum mechanics and

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:38.600
the fact that for the whole 20th century physicists
kind of punted the attempt to truly understand

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.109
what was going on in quantum mechanics.

00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:46.579
So something deeply hidden refers to Einstein's,
which I share that there's something going

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:49.670
on behind the operation of the natural world.

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:54.539
When we talk about quantum mechanics, we've
often not really pressed our best theoretical

00:00:54.539 --> 00:00:57.170
theories on what is happening.

00:00:57.170 --> 00:00:58.550
When we make a quantum measurement.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:02.289
I think it's time to actually face this problem
head on.

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:06.479
Why would it be that we don't press as much
as we might in that area?

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:08.880
Well, it's hard, you know.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:15.079
Um, the quantum mechanics is mysterious mostly
because it seems to imply that what truly

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:20.750
exists, the true nature of physical reality
is different from what we see when we look

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:21.750
at it.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:22.750
Right?

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:26.100
There's a difference between measurement outcomes
and physical reality, and that's something

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.289
that no other physical theory has ever featured.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:34.811
So way back in the 1920s and thirties, physicists
settled on a coping mechanism, which they

00:01:34.811 --> 00:01:40.790
called the Copenhagen interpretation, which
asked us to sort of not ask too carefully

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:43.759
what happens when you make a quantum measurement.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:46.350
And I think that we can do better than that
by now.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.580
How do you explain to lay people not only
in the simplest possible terms, what, what

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.000
quantum mechanics is, but also why it even
matters?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.049
Because I think sometimes there's a contingent
that when faced with something as complicated

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:06.229
as quantum mechanics, it can be easy to say,
if I don't even understand why that matters

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:09.550
to me or why that matters to human life, I'm
not even gonna think about it too much.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:10.550
Like why?

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:11.550
Why is this stuff important?

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:12.550
Yeah.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.950
Quantum mechanics is mysterious because we're
used to like seeing particles, right?

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.300
We say that there's the particle, it has a
position, it has a velocity, and quantum mechanics

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:23.360
says that's not what is really happening.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.500
Particles have a wave function and you can
observe their position, you can observe their

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:30.530
velocity, but that's not what is really going
on.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:36.170
And nevertheless, quantum mechanics is at
the center of all of modern physics.

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:40.810
It is the most important theory we have and
it is incredibly important to understand things

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:47.890
like Adam's electricity, transistors, lasers,
all of modern technology is based on quantum

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:49.540
mechanics in one way or another.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:54.680
To me, the most vivid fact about quantum mechanics
is that without it matter would be completely

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.180
unstable.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.220
Electrons would not stay orbiting the nuclei
of atoms.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.480
The chair you're sitting on would not be solid.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:04.870
It would all just collapse into a black hole
and a tiny fraction of a second.

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:09.340
So quantum mechanics is kind of really important
to how we understand the universe right now.

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:15.540
So to play pseudo intellectual devil's advocate,
is it important to understand those things

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:16.790
for the lay person?

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.950
As long as they work,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.000
you know, you don't have to understand anything
really to get through life except you know

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.920
how to eat and how to sleep and so forth.

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.560
But I think that what makes us human is something
very different, right?

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:33.290
I think that human beings like to do more
than simply survive.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:36.760
Of course, you can get through your daily
life as long as your daily life is not that

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:40.680
of being a physicist without knowing that
much about quantum mechanics.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.980
But it is best theory we have of the fundamental
nature of reality and it is understandable.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:51.670
So if you give into the human impulse to be
curious, to understand the world around us,

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:55.180
then understanding quantum mechanics should
be very high on your list of priorities.

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:56.180
Yeah.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:57.290
Very uh, very well said.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:04.040
Can you talk a little bit about some of the
ways in which a better understanding of quantum

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:09.270
mechanics could be a game changer over the
next, I mean, whatever timeline you think

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:10.270
is relevant.

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:14.250
I mean, whether it's 20 years or 50 years
or a hundred years, in what ways will a better

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:18.480
understanding of quantum mechanics change
the way in which humans interact with the

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:20.049
world around us in our environment?

00:04:20.049 --> 00:04:24.260
Well, I think that there's two big ways it's,
you know, it's very interesting to imagine

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:28.920
that we've been using quantum mechanics for
90 years or so and it's our best theory of

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.320
physics and yet we don't understand it.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.510
There are other things we also don't understand.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:38.250
For example, we don't understand how gravity
fits into the quantum mechanical paradigm

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:43.560
and no one seems to have, I had the idea that
maybe the fact that we don't understand quantum

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:48.450
gravity is related to the fact that we don't
understand quantum mechanics itself.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:52.890
So I think that understanding quantum mechanics
itself will be really, really helpful in our

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:57.670
pursuit of a theory of everything once and
for all final theory of the way the world

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:01.340
works at a much more down to earth level.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:05.500
Technology is improving to the point where
quantum mechanical effects are becoming manifest

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:07.460
in the devices that we build.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:12.750
We are building not only quantum computers,
but quantum cryptography, quantum eavesdropping,

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:18.090
a whole bunch of applications of quantum mechanics,
and so we're going to be needing to know what

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.020
the rules are of the quantum world.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:24.500
We're going to need to do better than the
kind of slapdash recipe we put together the

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:25.730
1920

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:30.100
can you talk a little bit about what the many
worlds theory is?

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:34.630
Well, when you make a measurement of a quantum
system, the system can be in what is called

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:35.870
a superposition.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:37.460
It's neither here nor there.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:42.470
It's in the superposition of every possible
place it could be, but when we look at it,

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:44.460
we never see a superposition.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:49.050
We see every single possible measurement outcome,
but only one at a time.

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.400
And so what many worlds says is what you're
missing.

00:05:51.400 --> 00:05:57.010
The trick is to realize that you yourself
are a quantum mechanical system.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:01.570
That means that you can be in super positions,
so when you measure a particle and it could

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:06.889
have many different possible locations, it's
not according to many worlds that once you

00:06:06.889 --> 00:06:10.139
observe it, it collapses to a single location.

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:16.230
Rather the particle is still in many possible
locations, but you are in many possible measurement

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.230
outcomes.

00:06:17.230 --> 00:06:21.680
There's a world where the particle was there
and you saw it there.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:25.770
There's another world where the particle is
over here and you saw it over here and this

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:30.110
is a straightforward prediction of the equations
of quantum mechanics.

00:06:30.110 --> 00:06:35.430
It's sort of been lurking in implicitly in
the formalism since the Schrodinger equation

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:40.240
came along in the 1920s and we just need to
face up that reality.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:44.920
Now is this for people who are hearing and
saying that, is this a multi-verse sort of

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:50.710
theory, the idea that I'm married to person
a in the world in which you know, you and

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:54.270
I are currently speaking, but there's another
world in which I'm not married.

00:06:54.270 --> 00:06:56.860
There's a third world in which a, I'm a tiger.

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:00.120
I mean, you know, however far you want to
take it and and sort of cartoonish, is it

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.460
concurrent with that idea or is it actually
something a little different?

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:06.000
It is a version of that idea.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.540
Absolutely, but it's not just that there is
every possible universe.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:10.790
There are certain universes.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:16.230
The ones that arise because of how the laws
of physics play out and there are not other

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:22.530
universes, but there's a huge challenge that
is raised by many worlds because there's the

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.660
notion of personal identity that we have.

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:28.900
I mean, in the sentence you just said, you
said, is there a world in which I am a tiger?

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:32.810
Okay, so no, there's no world in which you're
a tiger.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:37.560
There might be another world in which other
things happened that created a tiger out of

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:39.610
the same descendants that you had, right?

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:44.581
Or there's another world in which there's
a version very similar to you in which you

00:07:44.581 --> 00:07:47.940
had something different for dinner last night
or married a different person or something

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:48.940
like that.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:49.960
But they're not you.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:52.840
These people in the other worlds are all separate.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:57.669
Every person in a world as an individual and
all the individuals are different people.

00:07:57.669 --> 00:08:03.740
So I think sometimes when people think about
the multiverse theories, they share an identity

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:09.449
with that quote, other version of themselves
that is in some of these other universes.

00:08:09.449 --> 00:08:14.639
That seems, as I hear you saying, it may be
a slightly overly simplistic way of thinking

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:15.639
about it.

00:08:15.639 --> 00:08:16.639
Yeah.

00:08:16.639 --> 00:08:20.080
I think that, you know, the way the philosophers
would say it is we need to update our metaphysics

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:22.729
in order to take into account how many worlds
works.

00:08:22.729 --> 00:08:27.270
If I'm going to measure the spin of an electron
and I picked that because there's only two

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:31.510
possible outcomes, it's spinning counterclockwise
or it's spinning clockwise, right, so then

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:33.659
there will be two people afterward.

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:38.369
One person saw it's been clockwise, the other
solid spin counterclockwise, and it's very,

00:08:38.369 --> 00:08:43.479
very tempting for us to say, before we do
the measurement, I wonder which one I'm going

00:08:43.479 --> 00:08:47.769
to be, but the correct answer is there's no
answer to that question.

00:08:47.769 --> 00:08:50.220
There is no one that you're going to be.

00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:52.069
You are now one person.

00:08:52.069 --> 00:08:54.559
You will have two descendants a few moments
from now.

00:08:54.559 --> 00:08:55.730
They will be separate people.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.910
They'll be related to you in the same way
that an ordinary, older version of yourself

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.200
is really to the younger version of yourself.

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:03.980
It's just that there are multiple copies of
them.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:08.079
It's a different but completely sensible way
of thinking about reality.

00:09:08.079 --> 00:09:12.300
So along these lines, I want to talk about
entropy a little bit.

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:20.199
Um, one analogy that I have found useful in
talking about entropy is to explain, and I'm

00:09:20.199 --> 00:09:25.040
curious to your feedback on this as a science
educator, the idea that if you build a house

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:30.879
and leave it there and leave it subject to
weather and everything else that is going

00:09:30.879 --> 00:09:35.490
on around it, there could be a hurricane that
will knock some pieces off of it and it might

00:09:35.490 --> 00:09:40.300
snow a lot and that would damage of the roof,
which would fall off and eventually the house

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:42.209
would basically be rubble.

00:09:42.209 --> 00:09:48.420
You are not going to then have those same
or other forces happen to get the house back

00:09:48.420 --> 00:09:51.320
into the built condition in which it started.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:54.069
Is that a useful analogy to think about?

00:09:54.069 --> 00:09:55.069
Entropy?

00:09:55.069 --> 00:09:57.339
Yeah, no, that's an, that's not just an analogy.

00:09:57.339 --> 00:09:59.300
That's an example of entropy and action.

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:00.300
There you go.

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:04.889
And so when we think about that as a concept
more broadly, not applied to a house but applied

00:10:04.889 --> 00:10:11.170
to our universe, what are the sort of important
aspects of, of how and and why it matters?

00:10:11.170 --> 00:10:16.660
Well that nothing matters more to reality,
into life and to our existence here on earth.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:21.990
Then the fact that the entropy, the disorderliness
of the universe was very, very low near the

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.920
big bang 14 billion years ago and has been
rising ever since.

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.860
The fact that we're all born young and we
grow older with time we age, that's ultimately

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:32.769
because entropy is increasing.

00:10:32.769 --> 00:10:36.550
The fact that you remember the past and you
don't remember the future.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.420
That's ultimately because entropy is increasing.

00:10:38.420 --> 00:10:42.670
The fact that you can make a choice right
now, that will effect what happens in the

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:47.930
future, but you can not make a choice right
now that affected what did happen in the past.

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:49.660
All is because entropy is [inaudible].

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:56.610
Okay, so that may seem significant sort of
to the individual life, but why is that so

00:10:56.610 --> 00:11:00.560
significant when we think about the past and
future of the universe?

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:05.680
Well, we think we're not absolutely sure about
this, but we think that the fundamental laws

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:10.329
of physics don't actually distinguish between
the past and future.

00:11:10.329 --> 00:11:15.749
If you look at Newton's laws that we sometimes
learn in our physics courses, or if you look

00:11:15.749 --> 00:11:20.309
at the Schrodinger equation of quantum mechanics
or Einstein's equation of general relativity,

00:11:20.309 --> 00:11:24.139
none of them have a preference for one direction
of time over the other.

00:11:24.139 --> 00:11:28.240
They treat them equally, but the real world
seems to have a very strong preference.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:33.149
And we think that the origin of that strong
preference in the real world is because entropy

00:11:33.149 --> 00:11:34.459
was low.

00:11:34.459 --> 00:11:38.089
So if it wasn't for that difference, if it
wasn't for the fact that entropy used to be

00:11:38.089 --> 00:11:42.939
low, we would treat the past and future on
completely equal grounds.

00:11:42.939 --> 00:11:47.709
We would not remember the past any more than
we currently remember the future.

00:11:47.709 --> 00:11:48.779
Absolutely.

00:11:48.779 --> 00:11:50.360
A fascinating Sean.

